record.content:
Euler's Moment Equations in terms of the principle axis is given by

\begin{equation}
M_x = I_x \dot{\omega_x} + (I_z - I_y) \omega_y \omega_z
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
M_y = I_y \dot{\omega_y} + (I_x - I_z) \omega_x \omega_z 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
M_z = I_z \dot{\omega_z} + (I_y - I_x) \omega_x \omega_y
\end{equation}

In order to derive these equations, we start with the angular momentum of a rigid body

\begin{equation}
\vec{H_B} = I \omega = 
\left [ \begin{array}{c c c}  
I_{xx} & -I_{xy} & -I_{xz} \\
-I_{yx} & I_{yy} & -I_{yz} \\
-I_{zx} & -I_{zy} & I_{zz} 
\end{array} \right ]
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\omega_x \\
\omega_y \\
\omega_z
\end{array} \right ]
\end{equation}

Since the vector is in the body frame and we want the Moment in an inertial frame we need to use the transport theorem since our body is in a non-inertial reference frame to express the derivative of the angular momentum vector in this frame.  So the Moment is given by
\begin{equation}
\vec{M} = \dot{\vec{H_I}} = \dot{\vec{H_B}} + \vec{\omega} \times \vec{H_B}
\end{equation}

Since we are assuming the Inertia Tensor is expressed using the principal axis of the body the Products of Inertia are zero
\begin{equation}
I_{yx} = I_{xy} = I_{xz} = I_{zx} = I_{zy} = I_{yz} = 0
\end{equation}

and using the shorter notation 
\begin{equation}
I_{xx} = I_x 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
I_{yy} = I_y 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
I_{zz} = I_z
\end{equation}
Also since the Moments of Inertia are constant, when we take the derivative of the Inertia Tenser it is zero, so

\begin{equation}
\dot{\vec{H_B}} = 
\left [ \begin{array}{c c c}  
I_x & 0 & 0 \\
0 & I_y & 0 \\
0 & 0 & I_z 
\end{array} \right ]
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\dot{\omega_x} \\
\dot{\omega_y} \\
\dot{\omega_z}
\end{array} \right ]
+
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\omega_x \\
\omega_y \\
\omega_z
\end{array} \right ]
\times
\left [ \begin{array}{c c c}  
I_x & 0 & 0 \\
0 & I_y & 0 \\
0 & 0 & I_z 
\end{array} \right ]
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\omega_x \\
\omega_y \\
\omega_z
\end{array} \right ]
\end{equation}

Carrying out the matrix multiplication
\begin{equation}
\dot{\vec{H_B}} = 
\left [ \begin{array}{c}  
I_x \dot{\omega_x} \\
I_y \dot{\omega_y} \\
I_z \dot{\omega_z}
\end{array} \right ]
+
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\omega_x \\
\omega_y \\
\omega_z
\end{array} \right ]
\times
\left [ \begin{array}{c}  
I_x \omega_x \\
I_y \omega_y \\
I_z \omega_z
\end{array} \right ]
\end{equation}

after evaluating the cross product, we are left with adding the vectors
\begin{equation}
\dot{\vec{H_B}} = 
\left [ \begin{array}{c}  
I_x \dot{\omega_x} \\
I_y \dot{\omega_y} \\
I_z \dot{\omega_z}
\end{array} \right ]
+
\left [\begin{array}{c}
\omega_y \omega_z (I_z - I_y) \\
\omega_x \omega_z (I_x - I_z) \\
\omega_x \omega_y (I_y - I_x)
\end{array} \right ]
\end{equation}

Once we add these vectors we are left with Euler's Moment Equations

\begin{equation}
M_x = I_x \dot{\omega_x} + (I_z - I_y) \omega_y \omega_z
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
M_y = I_y \dot{\omega_y} + (I_x - I_z) \omega_x \omega_z 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
M_z = I_z \dot{\omega_z} + (I_y - I_x) \omega_x \omega_y
\end{equation}
